{
  "image_name": "bcbb588543c07d9bb648584511e74c51.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how changes in transfer RNA composition may influence cancer development and metastasis. At the top, a normal cell is shown becoming a cancerous cell through a “transformation” step, apparently linked to shifts in specific tRNAs that decode different codons, illustrated with examples such as leucine- and arginine-related tRNAs.\n\nFrom that cancer cell, two major outcomes are highlighted. One branch emphasizes tumour growth: increased levels of an initiator methionine tRNA are associated with higher overall translation, which then supports cell proliferation and production of particular proteins. This suggests that altered translation capacity helps the primary tumour expand.\n\nThe other branch focuses on metastasis. Here, distinct tRNAs seem to promote translation of mRNAs enriched for certain codons. One example pairs glutamate and arginine tRNAs with transcripts rich in GAA- and CGG-type codons, and another shows isoleucine tRNAs linked to AUC- or AUA-rich messages. Ribosomes are drawn translating these codon-biased mRNAs, leading to “metastatic progression.”\n\nOverall, the image conveys that cancer progression is not driven only by gene expression in general, but also by selective availability of tRNAs that can boost translation of codon-matched growth- and metastasis-related programs.",
